---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  Probador,
  ParenthesesCounter,
} from "../../components/cave/jacket-you-never-wore-balanced-parentheses";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
import CheeseTick from "../../components/icons/CheeseTick.astro";
import CheeseCross from "../../components/icons/CheeseCross.astro";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="You Can't Take Off a Jacket You Never Wore"
  description="Solving balanced parentheses with a dressing room metaphor"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      It might sound odd at first, but there is a close relationship between
      trying on clothes and knowing if a sequence of parentheses is valid.
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <aside class="notes">
        Now look at these two expressions. They look similar. They use the same
        symbols. But one of them breaks.
      </aside>
      <div class="grid grid-rows-2 gap-40 text-center">
        <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> (()()) </code>
        <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> ())() </code>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <!-- Intro video here. -->
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <aside class="notes">
        We are given a line of parentheses. The question is simple: is this line
        well formed? Every opening parenthesis must be closed. And nothing
        should close before it's opened.
      </aside>
      <div class="grid grid-rows-2 gap-40 text-center">
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 items-center gap-4">
          <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> (()()) </code>
          <div class="fragment fade-in text-6xl" data-fragment-index="1">
            <CheeseTick class="w-24 mx-auto" />
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="grid grid-cols-2 items-center gap-4">
          <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider"> ())() </code>
          <div class="fragment fade-in text-6xl" data-fragment-index="1">
            <CheeseCross class="w-24 mx-auto" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        The first thing we tried was counting. Parentheses come in pairs. So we
        count how many open, and how many close.
      </p>
      <span>()</span>
      <p>
        If the numbers match... maybe we are done. And in many cases, we are.
      </p>
      <span>(()())</span>
      <p>
        When the numbers do not match, the sequence is clearly invalid. If there
        are more openings than closings, something is missing.
      </p>
      <span>(()()</span>
      <p>If there are more closings than openings, something is extra.</p>
      <span>(()()))</span>
      <p>
        But then there is this. Same count. Nothing missing. Nothing extra. And
        still, completely broken. So counting helps. But it doesn't tell the
        whole story. Order matters.
      </p>
      <span>)(</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="count"
        title="Count Opening & Closing"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        So instead of counting at the end, we follow the line as it unfolds. We
        track how many openings are still pending. Closing more than we have
        opened means the sequence is invalid.
      </p>
      <span>())</span>
      <p>
        If we reach the end and some openings are still pending, the sequence is
        also invalid.
      </p>
      <span>(()</span>
      <p>
        This works well for a single type of parenthesis. But what if we have
        more than one type? We have round parentheses, brackets, and curly
        braces.
      </p>
      <span>([{}])</span>
      <p>
        It might seem that it works the same way. But look at this one. So order
        matters, and type matters.
      </p>
      <span>(]</span>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="balance"
        title="Tracking Pending to Close"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        We can track how many openings of each type are waiting to be closed.
        <span>(]</span>
        With this approach, our previous example is no longer a problem. However,
        it's easy to find a new issue.
      </p>
      <span>([)]</span>
      <p>
        We are trying to close a parenthesis when there is still an unclosed
        bracket. So we need to remember the most recent unclosed opener.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="multi-count"
        title="Counter Per Type"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's use an analogy to make this idea clearer. Think of a changing
        room. You walk in, and you put one t-shirt on. Then a sweater. Then a
        jacket. After looking at yourself in the mirror, you start taking your
        clothes off. You have to take off the jacket first. Then the sweater.
        Then the t-shirt.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="none"
        showTypeCounters={false}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's try a different sequence. T-Shirt, Sweater, Jacket, and... it's
        not possible to take off the sweater before the jacket. The last thing
        you put on is the first thing you take off.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
        ]}
        displayMode="none"
        showTypeCounters={false}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        This is exactly how our problem works. Instead of stacking clothes, we
        stack openings. Each time we see a closing one, it must match the most
        recent opening. We repeat this until the end, making sure we finish in
        the same state we started.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="none"
        showParentheses={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        This pile of symbols is what we call a stack. The last item added to the
        stack is the first one to be removed. Last In, First Out.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="stack"
        showParentheses={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      <p>
        Let's see how this works in Python code. We start with an empty stack.
        This is where we keep the parentheses that are still open. In Python, we
        implement the stack as a list.
      </p>
      <p>
        We also define a mapping from each closing symbol to the opening one it
        matches. That mapping tells us exactly what we expect to find on the
        stack when a closing appears.
      </p>
      <p>We then go through the input line, one character at a time.</p>
      <p>
        If the character is an opening, we push it onto the stack. Otherwise, we
        treat it as a closing one. We assume the input contains no letters,
        numbers, or other symbols.
      </p>
      <p>
        At that point, we ask: do we have something to close? If the stack is
        empty, we stop immediately.
      </p>
      <p>
        If there is something to close, we take the most recent opening. We use
        pop to get and remove the last item from the stack.
      </p>
      <p>
        We then use the mapping to check if the opening we pop matches the
        closing we just encountered. If it doesn't match, the sequence is
        invalid.
      </p>
      <p>
        We repeat this until we reach the end of the line. At the very end, only
        one thing matters: did we close everything we opened?
      </p>
      <p>
        The time complexity is linear because we process each character exactly
        once. The space complexity is also linear in the worst case, as we may
        need to store every opening if none are closed. That gives us an
        efficient solution.
      </p>
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def is_valid_parentheses(text: str) -> bool:
    # STEP: We create empty stack to remember opening parentheses
    stack = []
    # STEP: Mapping of closing to opening parentheses
    matching = {
        ')': '(',
        ']': '[',
        '}': '{',
    }

    # STEP: Process each character in the input text
    for char in text:
        # STEP: If we see an opening parenthesis, we remember it
        if char in '([{':
            stack.append(char)
        # STEP: If we see a closing parenthesis
        else:  # we assume char in ')]}'
            # STEP: There must be something to close
            if not stack:
                return False

            # STEP: It must close the last opened parenthesis
            last_open = stack.pop()

            # STEP: And it must be the correct type
            if matching[char] != last_open:
                return False

    # STEP: At the end, nothing should be left open
    return len(stack) == 0`}
      `
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      The key takeaway is simple. With one type of parenthesis, counting works.
      But with multiple types, we need to remember the order in which they were
      opened. A stack is the perfect data structure for this job.
    </aside>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Counter vs Stack</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-50 text-xl mt-4">
        <div class="text-center fragment">
          <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Single type</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">Just count!</p>
          <code class="text-green-400 text-3xl block mt-4">( ( ) ( ) )</code>
        </div>
        <div class="text-center fragment">
          <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Multiple types</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">Need a stack!</p>
          <code class="text-3xl block mt-4">
            <span class="text-green-400">(</span>
            <span class="text-blue-400">[</span>
            <span class="text-purple-400">{`{`}</span>
            <span class="text-purple-400">{`}`}</span>
            <span class="text-blue-400">]</span>
            <span class="text-green-400">)</span>
          </code>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="follow-up-code" class="text-lg">
    <aside class="notes">
      There is another way to solve this. Same idea. Different perspective. I'll
      leave it here... Leave a comment if you can figure out why it works.
    </aside>
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def is_valid_parentheses(text: str) -> bool:
    stack = []

    for char in text:
        if char in "([{":
            stack.append(char)
        else:
            if not stack:
                return False

            last_open = stack.pop()

            diff = ord(char) - ord(last_open)
            if diff != 1 and diff != 2:
                return False

    return len(stack) == 0
`}
    />
  </section>

  <section>
    <aside class="notes">
      This was today's Cheese Byte. Remember, the code matters, but the idea
      comes first.
    </aside>
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
